  "lines": [
    {
      "id": "l1",
      "text": "In eighteen fifty-two, a clerk in Calcutta finished the sums that found the tallest mountain on Earth."
    },
    {
      "id": "l2",
      "text": "He never saw it."
    },
    {
      "id": "l3",
      "text": "The peak stood a hundred and eighty kilometres away, behind a closed border."
    },
    {
      "id": "l4",
      "text": "So they measured it without going there. With triangles."
    },
    {
      "id": "l5",
      "text": "Six observations, from plains barely sixty metres up. The air bent the light by six minutes of arc. The mountain was less than one."
    },
    {
      "id": "l6",
      "text": "Radhanath Sikdar took the mean. Twenty-nine thousand and two feet."
    },
    {
      "id": "l7",
      "text": "Waugh announced it in eighteen fifty-six. They named it Everest, after a man who never saw it."
    },
    {
      "id": "l8",
      "text": "It already had names. Chomolungma. Sagarmatha."
    }
  ],
